course: biology
lesson: 309
title: Frogs and toads near home: spawn, croaking choruses, toad crossings and the fire-bellied toad
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In March a garden pond can fill with clumps of jelly dotted with black eggs: common frog spawn. A week or so later, long strings of eggs wound round the weeds in deeper water show that toads have come too.
explain: On a mild wet spring night you may hear the pond croaking and purring as males call for partners, and on the roads nearby toads walk back to the very pond where they hatched, which is why some roads have toad signs, tunnels and volunteers with buckets.
explain: In Britain the common frog is smooth, damp, long-legged and hops, while the common toad is dry, warty and walks; but «frog» and «toad» are loose everyday words, not strict science groups.
explain: A thousand eggs may become only a few adult frogs, because fish, newts, birds, beetles and dragonfly larvae eat so many. A small pond without fish, with gently sloping edges, gives them their best chance.
fact: Common frog spawn comes in a clump, each egg in its own jelly ball that protects it and keeps it warm like a little greenhouse; one common frog can lay about 1,000 to 2,000 eggs.
fact: Toadspawn comes in long strings like a necklace with a double row of eggs, wrapped around plants in deeper water; one string can be a few metres long.
fact: Out of a thousand eggs only a few become adult frogs, because fish, newts, birds, beetles and dragonfly larvae eat many, which is why frogs lay so many eggs.
fact: Male frogs and toads call to attract partners and tell rivals «this is my spot»; many puff up a vocal sac like a balloon to make the call louder, and natterjack toads are reported to be heard over a kilometre away, while common frogs purr quietly.
fact: In Britain, common frogs have smooth damp skin and long legs and hop, while common toads have dry warty skin and shorter legs and walk; toads carry bad-tasting poison in bumps behind their eyes.
fact: Toads do not give you warts; warts on people are caused by a virus, and the toad's bumps are glands, not warts. Wash your hands after touching any frog or toad, and wet your hands first to protect its skin.
fact: In spring toads walk back to the pond where they hatched, sometimes crossing roads; volunteers carry them across in buckets on mild wet nights, and some roads have small toad tunnels.
fact: Frogs and toads spend winter in compost heaps and under logs, and some male frogs at the bottom of ponds, breathing through their skin in the cold water.
fact: Fire-bellied toads of Europe and Asia have a green or dark back and an orange or red belly; when threatened they arch their back to show the belly, the unken reflex, warning that their skin makes bad-tasting slime.
fact: Fire-bellied toads have pupils shaped like a triangle or a heart; Oriental fire-bellied toads are popular pets, and hands must be washed after touching them.
fact: A small pond without fish, with shallow sloping edges, invites frogs, because fish eat spawn and tadpoles and sloping edges let froglets climb out; frogs often find a new pond on their own within a year or two.
exercise: What does common frog spawn look like? | A clump of jelly, each egg in its own ball | Long strings of jelly wound round the pond weeds | Single eggs stuck under leaves | Hard white eggs buried in the sand at the edge
exercise: What does the jelly around frog eggs do? | Protects them and keeps them warm like a greenhouse | Feeds the adult frogs | Makes the eggs sink down into the mud at the bottom | Stops the eggs from hatching
exercise: About how many eggs can one common frog lay? | About 1,000 to 2,000 | About 10 | About 100,000 to 200,000 | Just 2
exercise: What does toadspawn look like? | Long strings with a double row of eggs | A round clump of jelly, floating at the top | Single eggs on the surface | Green balls on the bank
exercise: Why do frogs lay so many eggs? | Most eggs and tadpoles are eaten, so only a few grow up | They like counting them | All of them always become frogs, and the pond needs that many | They need the jelly to keep warm
exercise: Why do male frogs and toads call in spring? | To attract partners and warn rivals off their spot | To tell the time to the other frogs in the pond | To scare away the rain | To call their mothers home
exercise: What helps many frogs make their calls louder? | A vocal sac that puffs up like a balloon | A snail shell they hold up to their mouth | Clapping their front feet | Shouting through a hollow reed
exercise: Which British animal is reported to be heard over a kilometre away? | The natterjack toad | The common garden frog | The fire-bellied toad | The see-through glass frog
exercise: How can you tell a British common toad from a common frog? | The toad is dry and warty and walks; the frog is smooth and hops | The toad is smooth and hops; the frog is warty and walks | The toad is always bright green and the frog always dark brown | The toad lives in trees and the frog in ponds
exercise: Where does a common toad keep its bad-tasting poison? | In bumps behind its eyes | In its long sticky tongue | In its toes | In its spawn only, to protect the eggs
exercise: Do toads give people warts? | No, warts are caused by a virus | Yes, if you touch them at all, even once | Yes, but only at night | No, but frogs do
exercise: Why do toads cross roads in spring? | They walk back to the pond where they hatched | They are chasing cars | They are moving to the sea for the summer | They are looking for warm tarmac to sleep on
exercise: How do some frogs at the bottom of a pond breathe in winter? | Through their skin in the cold water | Through a long reed straw to the surface | They hold one big breath all winter long | Through new gills that they grow again each autumn
exercise: What is the unken reflex of a fire-bellied toad? | Arching its back to show its bright warning belly | Jumping as high as it can | Puffing itself up to twice its usual size | Playing dead on its back for a whole day and night
exercise: What shape are a fire-bellied toad's pupils? | Like a triangle or a heart | Perfectly round like ours | Like a long slit like a cat's | Square like a box
exercise: Why should a frog pond have no fish? | Fish eat spawn and tadpoles | Fish make the water too warm | Frogs eat all the fish in the pond | Fish scare away the rain clouds
exercise: Why should a garden pond have gently sloping edges? | So froglets can climb out | So the water drains away quickly | So fish can sunbathe on the edge | So ducks cannot land on the water
test: Count them: a frog laid 1,000 eggs and only 5 grew into frogs. How many did not make it? | 995 | 1,005 | 500 | 905
test: Count them: a volunteer carries 3 buckets with 4 toads in each across the road. How many toads, and how many legs? | 12 toads and 48 legs | 12 toads and 24 legs | 7 toads and 28 legs | 12 toads and 72 legs
test: Count them: «the toad tunnel saved 9, no wait, 11 toads on Tuesday, and 6 on Wednesday». How many on the two days? | 17 | 15 | 26 | 20
test: «mum says don't touch the toad or ya get warts» What is the best reply? | Toads don't cause warts, a virus does; just wash your hands after | She is right, every toad gives you warts if you touch it | Only green toads give warts | Warts come from frogspawn
test: In a cartoon, a princess kisses a warty toad and gets warts all over. What is the truth? | Warts on people come from a virus, not from toads | Toads pass on their warts to anyone who touches them | Only frogs pass warts | Kissing gives toads warts
test: You find long strings of eggs round the weeds in deep water. «frogspawn innit?» What is right? | No, strings mean toadspawn; frogspawn comes in clumps | Yes, frogspawn always comes in long strings like these | No, they are fish eggs | No, they are snail trails
test: What would happen if someone put goldfish into a small frog pond? | Fewer tadpoles would survive, as fish eat spawn and tadpoles | More frogs would grow, because the fish keep the water clean | The frogs would eat the goldfish | Nothing would change
test: «why can't i go on toad patrol by myself at night, it's just toads» What is the reason? | Roads at night are dangerous, so go only with grown-ups in bright jackets | Toads bite children who pick them up | Toads only cross in daytime | Volunteers must be over 80
test: A fire-bellied toad suddenly arches its back and shows a red belly. What is it saying? | Stay away, my skin tastes bad | Please feed me now, I am hungry | I am too hot | I want to play
test: You are clearing the compost heap in January and find a cold, still frog. What should you do? | Put it back gently; it is spending the winter there | Take it inside to warm up by the fire | Throw it in the pond to wake it | Put it in the fridge to keep it cold and safe till spring
test: A friend picks up a bumpy, dry, slow-walking animal by a British pond and calls it a frog. What is it most likely? | A common toad | A common pond frog | A great crested newt | A little glass frog
exam: Identify the characteristic by which the spawn of the common toad may be distinguished from that of the common frog. | It is laid in long strings bearing a double row of eggs | It is laid in a single rounded clump of jelly near the surface | It is laid singly upon the surface of the water | It is laid within burrows upon dry land
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the transmission of warts. | Warts in humans are caused by a virus and are not transmitted by toads | Warts in humans are transmitted exclusively by contact with toads | Warts in humans are caused by contact with frogspawn | Warts in humans are caused by the poison glands of frogs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the unken reflex of the fire-bellied toad? | An arching of the back that displays a brightly coloured belly as a warning | A sudden leap into deep water to escape predators | The inflation of the vocal sac to produce a loud call audible across the marsh | The burial of the toad within mud during the winter
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a garden pond intended for frogs ought to contain no fish. | Fish consume the spawn and tadpoles of frogs | Fish raise the temperature of the water excessively | Fish are consumed in excessive numbers by adult frogs | Fish obstruct the mating calls of male frogs at night
